onto the actual surface of the moon. The agency just signed a $180.4 million contract with intuitive

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.540
machines of Houston to deliver seven payloads on a future commercial land. Five of those instruments

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:38.000
are funded directly by NASA, and together they'll help us better understand the moon's chemical

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.660
composition and internal structure. This is all part of the steadily growing commercial lunar

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:48.300
payload services program, or CLPS. I find it really encouraging to see how commercial companies have

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:54.520
moved from being occasional helpers to becoming essential partners in lunar exploration. Every successful

00:04:54.520 --> 00:05:00.200
landing builds confidence lowers costs and lets scientists focus on the questions rather than the rocket

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:05.960
science. It feels like we're watching a genuine ecosystem take shape. Of course, not everything in the

