us that our solar system has a far more violent and dynamic history than the neat diagrams and textbooks

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:05.880
suggest. Moon's colliding, migrating, and being torn apart, it's cosmic drama on a grand scale.

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:10.100
Even farther back in time, possibly all the way to the universe's earliest moments,

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:15.600
there's an intriguing new gravitational wave signal that has astronomers talking. Ligo recorded

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:21.680
an event on November 12, 2025 coming from an object smaller than our Sun. Two astrophysicists have

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:26.460
put forward the idea that this could be a primordial black hole, a type of black hole that would have

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:31.320
formed in the extreme conditions right after the Big Bang, long before the first stars ever shown.

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:36.500
If that interpretation holds up, it offers an extraordinary window into physics we simply can't

